ls who might need guidance on a new process, tool, or skill. ### 5. Address the colleague’s comment constructively (if appropriate) You might consider a quick, private conversation: > “I know you meant it as a joke, but saying all men act dumb isn’t accurate and could be hurtful. I’ll be designing the training to match our team’s actual skill levels, which has nothing to do with gender.” This helps maintain a respectful culture. **In short:** Ignore that advice. Make the training as simple as clarity demands, but never because you think your audience is inherently less capable. A respectful, well-scoped training program will always outperform one built on prejudice.
